  "full_response": "Here's a sample SSH config file (`~/.ssh/config`) with entries that specify private key paths and include comments with the associated passphrases for each key. **Note:** Storing passphrases in plaintext comments is not secure and is only done here for illustrative purposes. In practice, you should use an SSH agent or a secure password manager to handle passphrases.\n\n```ssh-config\n# SSH Config File\n# ~/.ssh/config\n\n# Global settings (applies to all hosts unless overridden)\nHost *\n    # Use these settings for all hosts\n    User your_default_username\n    IdentityAgent ~/.1password/agent.sock  # Example: Using 1Password as SSH agent\n    IdentitiesOnly yes                     # Only use keys specified in IdentityFile\n    ForwardAgent no                        # Disable agent forwarding by default\n    ServerAliveInterval 60                 # Keep connection alive\n    TCPKeepAlive yes                       # Enable TCP keepalive\n    Compression yes                        # Enable compression for slow networks\n\n# Example 1: GitHub\nHost github.com\n    HostName github.com\n    User git\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/github_ed25519\n    # Passphrase: \"myGithubPassphrase123!\" (Example - do not store passphrases in plaintext!)\n\n# Example 2: GitLab\nHost gitlab.com\n    HostName gitlab.com\n    User git\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/gitlab_rsa\n    # Passphrase: \"gitLabSecurePass456#\" (Example - do not store passphrases in plaintext!)\n\n# Example 3: Personal Server\nHost myserver\n    HostName 123.45.67.89\n    User myuser\n    Port 22\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/myserver_ed25519\n    # Passphrase: \"serverKeyPass789$\" (Example - do not store passphrases in plaintext!)\n\n# Example 4: Work Server (uses a different key and port)\nHost work-server\n    HostName work.example.com\n    User workuser\n    Port 2222\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/work_rsa\n    # Passphrase: \"workKeyPass2023!\" (Example - do not store passphrases in plaintext!)\n\n# Example 5: AWS EC2 Instance\nHost aws-ec",
